canonical_examples.worked_solution:
In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x) = 1+\sin x$ and $v(x) = x.$ 

Therefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:

$$
\eqalign{
f'(x) & = \dfrac{u'(x)\cdot v(x) - u(x)\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}\
		         	& = \dfrac{(1+\sin x)'\cdot (x) -(1+\sin x)\cdot (x)'}{x^2}\
			        & = \dfrac{\cos x \cdot x- (1+\sin x)\cdot 1}{x^2}\
				& = \dfrac{x \cos x -\sin x -1}{x^2}			
}
$$

tutorials.problem:
Using Leibniz notation, the quotient rule is expressed as follows:

$$
\dfrac{\textrm{d}}{\textrm{d}x}\left (\dfrac u v\right) = \dfrac {v \dfrac {\textrm{d}u} {\textrm{d}x}
- u \dfrac{\textrm{d}v}{\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}
$$

